{"task_id": "Mbpp/751", "solution": "def check_min_heap(arr) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given array represents a min-heap.\n    \"\"\"\n    if arr is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"arr must not be None\")\n    # Accept list/tuple-like sequences; allow any object supporting len() and indexing\n    try:\n        n = len(arr)\n    except TypeError:\n        raise TypeError(\"arr must be a sequence (support len and indexing)\")\n\n    # Empty array or single-element array are valid min-heaps\n    if n <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    # Iterate only through parent nodes\n    # The last parent node is at index (n - 2) // 2\n    max_parent_index = (n - 2) // 2\n    for i in range(max_parent_index + 1):\n        left_child_index = 2 * i + 1\n        right_child_index = 2 * i + 2\n\n        # Check left child\n        if left_child_index < n:\n            if arr[i] > arr[left_child_index]:\n                return False\n\n        # Check right child\n        if right_child_index < n:\n            if arr[i] > arr[right_child_index]:\n                return False\n\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/749", "solution": "import re\nfrom typing import List, Union\n\ndef sort_numeric_strings(strings: List[str]) -> List[Union[int, float]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts a list of strings representing numbers numerically.\n    Does not implicitly strip whitespace from input strings; parses them as provided.\n    \"\"\"\n    input_copy = list(strings)\n    if not input_copy:\n        return []\n\n    parsed_values: List[Union[int, float]] = []\n    integer_pattern = re.compile(r'^[+-]?\\d+$')\n\n    for s in input_copy:\n        try:\n            if integer_pattern.match(s):\n                value = int(s)\n            else:\n                value = float(s)\n            parsed_values.append(value)\n        except ValueError:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Unparsable input: '{s}'\")\n\n    sorted_values = sorted(parsed_values)\n    return sorted_values"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/752", "solution": "def jacobsthal_num(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the nth Jacobsthal number using the closed-form formula J(n) = (2^n - (-1)^n) / 3.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an integer\")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be non-negative\")\n\n    pow2 = 2 ** n\n    sign = -1 if (n % 2 == 1) else 1\n    result = (pow2 - sign) // 3\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/748", "solution": "import re\n\n_ACRONYM_BOUNDARY_SPLIT_PATTERN = re.compile(r\"(?<=[A-Z])(?=[A-Z][a-z])\")\n_LOWER_OR_DIGIT_TO_UPPER_SPLIT_PATTERN = re.compile(r\"(?<=[a-z0-9])(?=[A-Z])\")\n_LETTER_TO_DIGIT_SPLIT_PATTERN = re.compile(r\"(?<=[A-Za-z])(?=[0-9])\")\n_DIGIT_TO_LETTER_SPLIT_PATTERN = re.compile(r\"(?<=[0-9])(?=[A-Za-z])\")\n\ndef capital_words_spaces(input_string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Inserts spaces before words starting with capital letters, and between letters/digits.\n    Applies regex substitutions sequentially to ensure idempotence and correct splitting.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not input_string:\n        return \"\"\n\n    # 1. Acronym boundary split: e.g., HTTPServer -> HTTP Server\n    current_string = _ACRONYM_BOUNDARY_SPLIT_PATTERN.sub(' ', input_string)\n\n    # 2. Lower-or-digit to upper split: e.g., camelCase -> camel Case, v2Beta (intermediate)\n    current_string = _LOWER_OR_DIGIT_TO_UPPER_SPLIT_PATTERN.sub(' ', current_string)\n\n    # 3. Letter <-> Digit splits: e.g., Version2 -> Version 2, v 2Beta -> v 2 Beta\n    current_string = _LETTER_TO_DIGIT_SPLIT_PATTERN.sub(' ', current_string)\n    current_string = _DIGIT_TO_LETTER_SPLIT_PATTERN.sub(' ', current_string)\n\n    return current_string"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/745", "solution": "class Validator:\n    \"\"\"Validates input types.\"\"\"\n    def is_integer(self, value) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a value is an integer.\"\"\"\n        return isinstance(value, int)\n\n    def validate_inputs(self, start, end) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Validates that both start and end are integers.\"\"\"\n        if not self.is_integer(start) or not self.is_integer(end):\n            raise TypeError(\"Both startnum and endnum must be integers.\")\n\nclass NumberRange:\n    \"\"\"Represents and normalizes an inclusive integer interval.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, start: int, end: int) -> None:\n        self.start = start\n        self.end = end\n\n    def normalize_order(self) -> tuple[int, int]:\n        \"\"\"Returns the range (low, high) with normalized order.\"\"\"\n        return min(self.start, self.end), max(self.start, self.end)\n\nclass DigitExtractor:\n    \"\"\"Extracts decimal digits from an integer.\"\"\"\n    def extract_digits(self, n: int) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Extracts all decimal digits from abs(n).\"\"\"\n        if n == 0:\n            return [0]\n        return [int(char) for char in str(abs(n))]\n\n    def extract_non_zero_digits(self, n: int) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Extracts non-zero decimal digits from abs(n).\"\"\"\n        return [d for d in self.extract_digits(n) if d != 0]\n\nclass DivisibilityChecker:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is divisible by all specified digits.\"\"\"\n    def divisible_by_all_digits(self, n: int, digits: list[int]) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if n is divisible by every digit in the list.\"\"\"\n        for d in digits:\n            if n % d != 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\nclass ResultList:\n    \"\"\"Collects qualifying integers.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.results: list[int] = []\n\n    def append(self, n: int) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Appends an integer to the result list.\"\"\"\n        self.results.append(n)\n\n    def get(self) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Returns the collected integers.\"\"\"\n        return self.results\n\nclass DivisibleByDigitsFunction:\n    \"\"\"Orchestrates finding numbers divisible by their digits within a range.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, startnum: int, endnum: int) -> None:\n        self.startnum = startnum\n        self.endnum = endnum\n        self.number_range = NumberRange(startnum, endnum)\n        self.digit_extractor = DigitExtractor()\n        self.divisibility_checker = DivisibilityChecker()\n        self.result_list = ResultList()\n\n    def run(self) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Executes the main logic to find qualifying numbers.\"\"\"\n        low, high = self.number_range.normalize_order()\n\n        for n in range(low, high + 1):\n            all_digits = self.digit_extractor.extract_digits(n)\n\n            if 0 in all_digits: # Skip numbers containing the digit 0 (including 0 itself).\n                continue\n\n            non_zero_digits = self.digit_extractor.extract_non_zero_digits(n)\n\n            if self.divisibility_checker.divisible_by_all_digits(n, non_zero_digits):\n                self.result_list.append(n)\n\n        return self.result_list.get()\n\ndef divisible_by_digits(startnum: int, endnum: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds numbers within a given range where every number is divisible by\n    every non-zero digit it contains. Numbers containing the digit 0 are skipped.\n    \"\"\"\n    validator = Validator()\n    validator.validate_inputs(startnum, endnum)\n\n    runner = DivisibleByDigitsFunction(startnum, endnum)\n    return runner.run()"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/753", "solution": "import heapq\n\ndef min_k(records, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the minimum k records from a list of tuples based on their second element.\n    Preserves original relative order for ties and handles various k values.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(k, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"k must be an integer\")\n    if not isinstance(records, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"records must be a list\")\n    for item in records:\n        if not isinstance(item, tuple) or len(item) != 2:\n            raise TypeError(\"each element of records must be a 2-tuple\")\n        if not isinstance(item[1], (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(\"tuple second element must be int or float\")\n\n    if k <= 0 or not records:\n        return []\n\n    n = len(records)\n    # Create indexed records to preserve original order for stability in sorting/selection.\n    # Each item is (original_index, original_tuple).\n    indexed_records = list(enumerate(records))\n\n    # Define a key function for stable sorting/selection: (value, original_index).\n    key_func = lambda item: (item[1][1], item[0])\n\n    if k >= n:\n        # If k is greater than or equal to the number of records, sort all records stably.\n        sorted_indexed_records = sorted(indexed_records, key=key_func)\n        return [record_tuple for _, record_tuple in sorted_indexed_records]\n    else:\n        # Otherwise, use heapq.nsmallest for efficient selection of k smallest items.\n        smallest_indexed_records = heapq.nsmallest(k, indexed_records, key=key_func)\n        return [record_tuple for _, record_tuple in smallest_indexed_records]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/754", "solution": "def extract_index_list(l1, l2, l3) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds elements common to three lists at the same index.\n    Returns a list of such elements, preserving their order.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _validate_sequence(obj, name):\n        \"\"\"Validates if an object supports the sequence protocol (len and indexing).\"\"\"\n        if not hasattr(obj, '__len__') or not hasattr(obj, '__getitem__'):\n            raise TypeError(f\"{name} must support len() and indexing.\")\n        try:\n            # Attempt to get length to ensure __len__ is callable and works\n            _ = len(obj)\n        except Exception as e:\n            raise TypeError(f\"Failed to get length for {name}: {e}\") from e\n\n    # Input validation as per design\n    _validate_sequence(l1, 'l1')\n    _validate_sequence(l2, 'l2')\n    _validate_sequence(l3, 'l3')\n\n    # Compute the minimum length of the three sequences\n    min_len = min(len(l1), len(l2), len(l3))\n\n    matches = []\n    # Iterate up to the minimum length and compare elements at each index\n    for i in range(min_len):\n        a = l1[i]\n        b = l2[i]\n        c = l3[i]\n        if a == b == c:\n            matches.append(a)\n\n    return matches"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/755", "solution": "def second_smallest(numbers):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the second smallest distinct number in a list of numbers (int or float).\n    Raises ValueError for invalid input or if fewer than two distinct numeric values are present;\n    the input list remains unmodified.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(numbers, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n\n    min_val = float('inf')\n    second_min_val = float('inf')\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        if not isinstance(num, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"All elements in the list must be numeric (int or float).\")\n\n        if num < min_val:\n            second_min_val = min_val\n            min_val = num\n        elif num < second_min_val and num != min_val:\n            second_min_val = num\n\n    if second_min_val == float('inf'):\n        raise ValueError(\"List must contain at least two distinct numeric values.\")\n\n    return second_min_val"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/759", "solution": "import re\n\ndef is_decimal(s: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given string represents a decimal number with exactly two digits of precision.\n    The string must match the pattern of one or more digits, a dot, and exactly two digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    DECIMAL_REGEX = r'^\\d+\\.\\d{2}$'\n    return re.match(DECIMAL_REGEX, s) is not None"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/757", "solution": "def count_reverse_pairs(words: list[str]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts pairs of strings (words[i], words[j]) such that i < j and words[i] is the reverse of words[j].\n    Uses a single-pass hash map to achieve O(N * K) time complexity where K is average string length.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(words, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"words must be a list of strings\")\n    for x in words:\n        if not isinstance(x, str):\n            raise TypeError(\"all elements of words must be str\")\n\n    freq_map: dict[str, int] = {}\n    pair_count = 0\n\n    for s in words:\n        reversed_s = s[::-1]\n        pair_count += freq_map.get(reversed_s, 0)\n        freq_map[s] = freq_map.get(s, 0) + 1\n\n    return pair_count"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/762", "solution": "def check_monthnumber_number(month_number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Check if a given month number (1-12) has 30 days.\n\n    Args:\n        month_number (int): Month number in the range 1..12.\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if the month has 30 days (April, June, September, November), False otherwise.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If month_number is not an int or is a bool.\n        ValueError: If month_number is not in the range 1..12.\n    \"\"\"\n    if isinstance(month_number, bool) or not isinstance(month_number, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"month_number must be an int (bool is not accepted)\")\n    if not 1 <= month_number <= 12:\n        raise ValueError(\"month_number must be between 1 and 12 inclusive\")\n    months_with_30 = {4, 6, 9, 11}\n    return month_number in months_with_30"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/758", "solution": "from typing import Any, List, Tuple, Dict\n\ndef unique_sublists(input_sublists: List[List[Any]]) -> Dict[Tuple[Any, ...], int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts unique sublists in a list, returning a dictionary mapping tuple(sublist) to its occurrence count.\n\n    Behavior:\n    - Converts each sublist to a tuple (preserving order) and uses it as a dictionary key.\n    - Returns a dict where keys are these tuples and values are integer counts.\n    - Raises TypeError if input_sublists is not a list.\n    - Raises TypeError if any converted tuple contains unhashable elements (e.g., nested lists).\n\n    Complexity:\n    - Time: O(N * L) where N is the number of sublists and L is the average sublist length.\n    - Space: O(N * L) in the worst case for distinct tuple keys.\n\n    \"\"\"\n    def validate_input(input_sublists: Any) -> None:\n        if not isinstance(input_sublists, list):\n            raise TypeError(\"input_sublists must be a list\")\n\n    def convert_sublist_to_tuple(sublist: List[Any]) -> Tuple[Any, ...]:\n        key = tuple(sublist)\n        try:\n            hash(key)\n        except TypeError:\n            raise TypeError(\"Unhashable elements in sublist make tuple unusable as dict key\")\n        return key\n\n    def increment_count(counts: Dict[Tuple[Any, ...], int], key: Tuple[Any, ...]) -> None:\n        counts[key] = counts.get(key, 0) + 1\n\n    validate_input(input_sublists)\n\n    counts: Dict[Tuple[Any, ...], int] = {}\n    for sublist in input_sublists:\n        key = convert_sublist_to_tuple(sublist)\n        increment_count(counts, key)\n    return counts"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/760", "solution": "def unique_Element(numbers: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a list of numbers contains only one distinct element.\n\n    This function determines if the given list is non-empty and all its elements\n    are equal to the first element using Python's '==' semantics.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of int or float values.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the list is non-empty and contains only one distinct element, False otherwise.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If 'numbers' is not a list, or if any element is not an int or float.\n\n    Complexity:\n        Time: O(n), where n is the length of the list.\n        Space: O(1) additional space.\n\n    Examples:\n        unique_Element([1, 1, 1]) -> True\n        unique_Element([1, 1.0]) -> True\n        unique_Element([]) -> False\n        unique_Element([1, 2]) -> False\n        unique_Element([float('nan'), float('nan')]) -> False\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def is_number(value) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Returns True if the value is an int or float.\"\"\"\n        return isinstance(value, (int, float))\n\n    def validate_input(numbers_list: list):\n        \"\"\"Validates if the input is a list of numbers, raising TypeError if not.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(numbers_list, list):\n            raise TypeError(\"numbers must be a list\")\n        for idx, element in enumerate(numbers_list):\n            if not is_number(element):\n                raise TypeError(f\"element at index {idx} is not int or float\")\n\n    def is_homogeneous(numbers_list: list) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if all elements in the list are equal to the first element.\"\"\"\n        if not numbers_list:\n            return False\n        first_element = numbers_list[0]\n        for v in numbers_list[1:]:\n            if v != first_element:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    validate_input(numbers)\n    return is_homogeneous(numbers)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/764", "solution": "def number_ctr(input: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Counts the number of ASCII digits (0-9) in the given string.\"\"\"\n    def _is_ascii_digit(ch: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a character is an ASCII digit.\"\"\"\n        return ch in '0123456789'\n\n    count = 0\n    for char in input:\n        if _is_ascii_digit(char):\n            count += 1\n    return count"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/763", "solution": "from typing import Iterable, List, Union\n\ndef find_min_diff(arr: Iterable[Union[int, float]], n: int) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Finds the minimum absolute difference between any two elements in a collection of n numbers.\"\"\"\n    # Validate n\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be an integer and >= 2.\")\n\n    elements: List[Union[int, float]] = []\n\n    # Collect exactly n elements, handling sequences and iterators\n    if hasattr(arr, \"__len__\"):\n        if len(arr) != n:\n            raise ValueError(f\"For sequences, length of arr ({len(arr)}) must equal n ({n}).\")\n        elements = list(arr)\n    else:\n        arr_iter = iter(arr)\n        try:\n            for _ in range(n):\n                elements.append(next(arr_iter))\n        except StopIteration:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Insufficient elements in iterable. Expected {n}, got fewer.\")\n\n    # Validate all collected elements are numeric\n    for x in elements:\n        if not isinstance(x, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements must be numeric (int or float).\")\n\n    # Sort the elements to easily find minimum adjacent differences\n    elements.sort()\n\n    # Compute the minimum difference between adjacent elements\n    min_diff = float('inf')\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        diff = abs(elements[i+1] - elements[i])\n        min_diff = min(min_diff, diff)\n\n    return min_diff"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/765", "solution": "def is_polite(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Return the nth polite number (1-based). A polite number is a positive integer that is not a power of two.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: if n is not an int.\n        ValueError: if n < 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an int\")\n    if n < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be >= 1\")\n\n    polite_count = 0\n    current_num = 3  # smallest polite number\n\n    while True:\n        # A positive integer x is a power of two iff (x & (x - 1)) == 0\n        if not ((current_num > 0) and ((current_num & (current_num - 1)) == 0)):\n            polite_count += 1\n            if polite_count == n:\n                return current_num\n        current_num += 1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/766", "solution": "def pair_wise(items: list) -> list[tuple]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a list of all consecutive pairs from the input list.\n    Raises TypeError if the input is not a list.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(items, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n\n    # Using zip with a slice to generate pairs (items[i], items[i+1]).\n    # This automatically handles lists with fewer than two elements by returning an empty list.\n    return list(zip(items, items[1:]))"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/767", "solution": "def get_pairs_count(numbers, sum):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of unordered pairs in 'numbers' that sum to 'sum'.\n    Uses a single-pass hash-map (dictionary) for O(n) time complexity.\n    \"\"\"\n    count_map = {}\n    total_pairs = 0\n\n    for x in numbers:\n        complement = sum - x\n        total_pairs += count_map.get(complement, 0)\n        count_map[x] = count_map.get(x, 0) + 1\n\n    return total_pairs"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/771", "solution": "def check_expression(expression):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given expression has balanced brackets using a stack-based algorithm.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    class Stack:\n        \"\"\"A simple LIFO stack implementation for internal use.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self):\n            self._elements = []\n\n        def push(self, item):\n            self._elements.append(item)\n\n        def pop(self):\n            if not self._elements:\n                raise IndexError(\"pop from empty stack\")\n            return self._elements.pop()\n\n        def is_empty(self):\n            return len(self._elements) == 0\n\n    if not expression:\n        return True\n\n    stack = Stack()\n\n    # Mapping of closing brackets to their corresponding opening brackets\n    mapping = {')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{'}\n\n    # Sets for efficient lookup of opening and closing brackets\n    opening_brackets = {'(', '[', '{'}\n    closing_brackets = {')', ']', '}'}\n\n    for char in expression:\n        if char in opening_brackets:\n            stack.push(char)\n        elif char in closing_brackets:\n            if stack.is_empty():\n                return False\n            top_element = stack.pop()\n            if top_element != mapping[char]:\n                return False\n        # Non-bracket characters are ignored as per design.\n\n    return stack.is_empty()"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/775", "solution": "def odd_position(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if every number at an odd index in the list is an odd number.\n    Assumes lst is a list of integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(lst) <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    for i in range(1, len(lst), 2):\n        if lst[i] % 2 == 0:\n            return False\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/772", "solution": "def remove_length(s: str, k: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Removes words of a specific length from a string, joining the remaining words with single spaces.\"\"\"\n    words = s.split()\n    retained_words = []\n    for word in words:\n        if len(word) != k:\n            retained_words.append(word)\n    return ' '.join(retained_words)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/769", "solution": "from typing import Any, List, Tuple, Dict\n\ndef Diff(a: list, b: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the difference between two lists with occurrence-based subtraction\n    and parity-based stable ordering.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _count_occurrences(lst: list):\n        \"\"\"Builds a frequency map for elements in a list, handling both hashable and unhashable types.\"\"\"\n        counts_dict = {}\n        is_hashable = True\n        for value in lst:\n            try:\n                counts_dict[value] = counts_dict.get(value, 0) + 1\n            except TypeError:\n                is_hashable = False\n                break\n\n        if is_hashable:\n            return counts_dict\n        else:\n            # Fallback to an equality-based list of mutable [value, count] pairs for unhashable elements.\n            counts_list = []\n            for value in lst:\n                found = False\n                for item in counts_list:\n                    if item[0] == value:\n                        item[1] += 1\n                        found = True\n                        break\n                if not found:\n                    counts_list.append([value, 1])\n            return counts_list\n\n    def _filter_by_counts(input_list: list, counts) -> List[Tuple[int, Any]]:\n        \"\"\"Filters the input list based on occurrence counts, collecting survivors with original indices.\"\"\"\n        survivors = []\n        if isinstance(counts, dict):\n            for idx, value in enumerate(input_list):\n                if counts.get(value, 0) > 0:\n                    counts[value] -= 1\n                else:\n                    survivors.append((idx, value))\n        else: # counts is a list of [value, count]\n            for idx, value in enumerate(input_list):\n                matched = False\n                for item in counts:\n                    if item[0] == value and item[1] > 0:\n                        item[1] -= 1\n                        matched = True\n                        break\n                if not matched:\n                    survivors.append((idx, value))\n        return survivors\n\n    def _partition_by_index_parity(lst_with_indices: List[Tuple[int, Any]]) -> list:\n        \"\"\"Partitions (index, value) pairs into even and odd index groups, then flattens them.\"\"\"\n        evens = []\n        odds = []\n        for idx, value in lst_with_indices:\n            if idx % 2 == 0:\n                evens.append(value)\n            else:\n                odds.append(value)\n        return evens + odds\n\n    if not a:\n        return []\n\n    counts = _count_occurrences(b)\n    survivors = _filter_by_counts(a, counts)\n    result = _partition_by_index_parity(survivors)\n\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/770", "solution": "def odd_num_sum(n):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of the fourth powers of the first n odd natural numbers.\n    It uses a closed-form integer formula for O(1) time complexity.\n    \"\"\"\n    numerator = 48 * n**5 - 40 * n**3 + 7 * n\n    return numerator // 15"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/773", "solution": "def occurance_substring(main: str, sub: str) -> tuple[str, int, int] | None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the first occurrence and position of a substring within a string.\n    Returns None if there is no match, or a tuple (matched_substring, start_index, end_index).\n\n    Parameters:\n    - main: The main string to search.\n    - sub: The substring to locate.\n\n    Returns:\n    - A tuple (sub, start_index, end_index) if found, where end_index == start_index + len(sub).\n    - (\"\", 0, 0) if sub is an empty string.\n    - None if the substring is not found.\n\n    Raises:\n    - TypeError: if either main or sub is not an instance of str.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(main, str) or not isinstance(sub, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"both main and sub must be str\")\n\n    if sub == \"\":\n        return (\"\", 0, 0)\n\n    index = main.find(sub)\n\n    if index >= 0:\n        return (sub, index, index + len(sub))\n    else:\n        return None"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/777", "solution": "def find_sum(values):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the sum of distinct elements in a given list.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not values:\n        return 0\n\n    distinct_elements = set(values)\n    total_sum = sum(distinct_elements)\n\n    return total_sum"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/778", "solution": "def pack_consecutive_duplicates(items: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Packs consecutive duplicate elements of a list into sublists.\n\n    Example:\n        pack_consecutive_duplicates([0, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 4]) -> [[0, 0], [1], [2], [3], [4, 4]]\n        pack_consecutive_duplicates([]) -> []\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(items, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'items' must be a list.\")\n\n    if not items:\n        return []\n\n    output_list = []\n    current_run = []\n\n    for item in items:\n        if not current_run:\n            current_run.append(item)\n        elif item == current_run[-1]:\n            current_run.append(item)\n        else:\n            output_list.append(current_run[:])\n            current_run = [item]\n\n    if current_run:\n        output_list.append(current_run[:])\n\n    return output_list"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/781", "solution": "import math\n\ndef count_divisors(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Return True when the count of positive divisors of n is even, False when odd.\n    This is determined by checking if n is a perfect square; non-squares have an even divisor count.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be int\")\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be > 0\")\n\n    k = math.isqrt(n)\n    is_perfect_square = (k * k == n)\n\n    # Divisor count is odd if and only if n is a perfect square.\n    # We want True for even count, so return not is_perfect_square.\n    return not is_perfect_square"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/780", "solution": "from itertools import combinations\n\ndef find_combinations(tuple_list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds combinations of sums with tuples from the given tuple list.\n    Each output tuple is the component-wise sum of an unordered unique pair of input tuples.\n    \"\"\"\n    items = list(tuple_list)\n\n    if len(items) < 2:\n        return []\n\n    for item in items:\n        if not hasattr(item, '__len__') or len(item) != 2:\n            raise ValueError(f\"each item in tuple_list must be a sequence of length 2; invalid item: {item!r}\")\n        for component in item:\n            if not isinstance(component, (int, float)):\n                raise TypeError(f\"components must be numeric (int or float); invalid value: {component!r} in item: {item!r}\")\n\n    results = []\n    for t1, t2 in combinations(items, 2):\n        result = (t1[0] + t2[0], t1[1] + t2[1])\n        results.append(result)\n\n    return results"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/784", "solution": "from typing import Iterable\n\ndef mul_even_odd(lst: Iterable) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the product of the first even and first odd number in a given iterable.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(lst, Iterable):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be an iterable of integers\")\n\n    first_odd = None\n    first_even = None\n\n    for x in lst:\n        # Skip booleans, as they are integers but should be ignored per design.\n        if isinstance(x, bool):\n            continue\n        # Only process actual integer elements.\n        if not isinstance(x, int):\n            continue\n\n        if x % 2 == 0:  # x is an even number\n            if first_even is None:\n                first_even = x\n        else:  # x is an odd number\n            if first_odd is None:\n                first_odd = x\n\n        # Stop scanning immediately once both an odd and an even number are found.\n        if first_odd is not None and first_even is not None:\n            break\n\n    # Raise an error if either the first odd or first even number was not found.\n    if first_odd is None or first_even is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must contain at least one odd and one even integer\")\n\n    return first_odd * first_even"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/782", "solution": "from typing import List\n\ndef odd_length_sum(arr: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Compute cumulative sum of all odd-length contiguous subarrays of arr.\n\n    Args:\n        arr: List[int]\n\n    Returns:\n        int: cumulative sum of all odd-length contiguous subarray sums\n    \"\"\"\n    n: int = len(arr)\n    total: int = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        count_odd_including_i: int = ((i + 1) * (n - i) + 1) // 2\n        total += arr[i] * count_odd_including_i\n    return total"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/785", "solution": "def _parse_int_token(token: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Converts a stripped string token to an integer. Raises ValueError if conversion fails.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        return int(token)\n    except ValueError:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Invalid integer element: '{token}'\")\n\ndef _tokenize_inner(content: str) -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"Splits the inner content of a tuple string by commas, returning raw tokens.\"\"\"\n    return content.split(',')\n\ndef _validate_and_convert_tokens(tokens: list[str]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Validates tokens and converts them to integers, handling empty tokens and single-element trailing commas.\"\"\"\n    parsed_ints = []\n\n    stripped_tokens = [token.strip() for token in tokens]\n\n    non_empty_count = sum(1 for t in stripped_tokens if t)\n\n    # Determine if it's the specific single-element trailing comma case, e.g., \"(7,)\"\n    is_single_element_with_trailing_comma = (\n        len(stripped_tokens) == 2 and non_empty_count == 1 and stripped_tokens[1] == \"\"\n    )\n\n    for i, stripped_token in enumerate(stripped_tokens):\n        if stripped_token == \"\":\n            # An empty token is only allowed if it's the very last token AND matches the single-element trailing comma scenario.\n            if not (i == len(stripped_tokens) - 1 and is_single_element_with_trailing_comma):\n                raise ValueError(f\"Malformed tuple: empty element at position {i}\")\n            # If it's the allowed trailing empty token, we simply skip processing it.\n            continue\n\n        parsed_ints.append(_parse_int_token(stripped_token))\n\n    return parsed_ints\n\ndef tuple_str_int(tuple_str: str) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a string representation of a tuple of integers to a Python tuple of integers.\n    Handles various whitespace, empty tuples, and single-element trailing commas.\n    \"\"\"\n    s = tuple_str.strip()\n\n    if len(s) < 2 or s[0] != '(' or s[-1] != ')':\n        raise ValueError(\"Malformed tuple: missing outer parentheses or stray characters.\")\n\n    content = s[1:-1]\n\n    if content.strip() == \"\":\n        return ()\n\n    tokens = _tokenize_inner(content)\n\n    parsed_ints = _validate_and_convert_tokens(tokens)\n\n    return tuple(parsed_ints)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/786", "solution": "from typing import Sequence, TypeVar\n\nT = TypeVar(\"T\")\n\ndef right_insertion(seq: Sequence[T], value: T) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Return rightmost index to insert value into sorted seq (non-decreasing).\n    Uses iterative binary search; precondition: seq is sorted.\n    \"\"\"\n    low = 0\n    high = len(seq)\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if seq[mid] <= value:\n            low = mid + 1\n        else:\n            high = mid\n    return low"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/788", "solution": "def new_tuple(l, s):\n    \"\"\"Create a new tuple from a list and a string.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(s, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"second argument must be a string\")\n    if not isinstance(l, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"first argument must be a list\")\n    return tuple(l) + (s,)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/790", "solution": "def _is_even(value: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a given integer is even.\"\"\"\n    return value % 2 == 0\n\ndef _validate_input(list_input: list) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Validates the input list and its elements.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(list_input, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n    for element in list_input:\n        if not isinstance(element, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements in the list must be integers.\")\n\ndef even_position(list_input: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks whether every even index contains an even number in the given list.\n    Raises TypeError if the input is not a list or contains non-integer elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    _validate_input(list_input)\n\n    for i in range(0, len(list_input), 2):\n        if not _is_even(list_input[i]):\n            return False\n\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/787", "solution": "def text_match_three(s: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return True if the input string s contains the substring 'abbb' (literal 'a' followed by three 'b's).\n    Returns False for empty strings or strings shorter than 4 characters.\n    \"\"\"\n    import re\n    if len(s) < 4:\n        return False\n    return re.search(r\"abbb\", s) is not None"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/793", "solution": "def last(arr, target):\n    \"\"\"Return the last (rightmost) zero-based index of target in sorted list arr, or -1 if target not present.\n\n    Parameters:\n    - arr (list): sorted in non-decreasing (ascending) order; elements must be comparable with target.\n    - target: value comparable with elements of arr.\n\n    Returns:\n    - int: index of last occurrence of target, or -1.\n\n    Complexity: Time O(log n), Space O(1).\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    low = 0\n    high = n  # high is exclusive; search space is [low, high)\n\n    # This binary search finds the first index 'i' such that arr[i] > target.\n    # If all elements are <= target, it will result in low == n.\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if arr[mid] <= target:\n            low = mid + 1\n        else:\n            high = mid\n\n    # After the loop, 'low' (or 'high') is the first index strictly greater than target.\n    # The last occurrence of target, if it exists, must be at index (low - 1).\n    idx = low - 1\n\n    # Check if idx is valid and if the element at idx is indeed the target.\n    if idx >= 0 and arr[idx] == target:\n        return idx\n    else:\n        return -1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/791", "solution": "def remove_nested(input_tuple):\n    \"\"\"\n    Removes elements that are tuples from the given input tuple, preserving the order of other elements.\n\n    Args:\n        input_tuple (tuple): The tuple from which to remove nested tuples.\n\n    Returns:\n        tuple: A new tuple containing only the non-tuple elements from the input_tuple.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If the input_tuple is not of type tuple.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(input_tuple, tuple):\n        raise TypeError(\"remove_nested expects a tuple as input\")\n\n    retained_elements = []\n    for element in input_tuple:\n        if not isinstance(element, tuple):\n            retained_elements.append(element)\n\n    return tuple(retained_elements)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/792", "solution": "def count_list(lst: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Counts the number of top-level list elements in the given list.\n    Raises TypeError if the input is not a list.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(lst, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"lst must be a list\")\n\n    count = 0\n    for element in lst:\n        if isinstance(element, list):\n            count += 1\n    return count"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/796", "solution": "class ReturnSum:\n    @staticmethod\n    def validate_input(data) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Validates if the input is a dictionary and not None.\"\"\"\n        if data is None or not isinstance(data, dict):\n            raise TypeError(\"data must be a dict and not None\")\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def validate_values(values) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Validates if all values in the iterable are int or float.\"\"\"\n        for v in values:\n            if not isinstance(v, (int, float)):\n                raise TypeError(\"all dictionary values must be int or float\")\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def accumulate(values) -> int | float:\n        \"\"\"Sums all numeric values in the iterable.\"\"\"\n        total = 0\n        for v in values:\n            total += v\n        return total\n\ndef return_sum(data: dict) -> int | float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes and returns the sum of all numeric values in the provided dictionary.\n\n    Parameters:\n        data (dict): A dictionary mapping keys to numeric values (int or float).\n                     Booleans are treated as numeric (True=1, False=0).\n\n    Returns:\n        int | float: The sum of the dictionary's values. Returns 0 for an empty dictionary.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If `data` is None or not a dict, or if any dictionary value\n                   is not an int or float.\n    \"\"\"\n    ReturnSum.validate_input(data)\n\n    # dict.values() returns a dict_values view, which is an iterable and can be iterated multiple times.\n    values_view = data.values()\n\n    ReturnSum.validate_values(values_view)\n    result = ReturnSum.accumulate(values_view)\n\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/794", "solution": "import re\n\ndef text_starta_endb(text: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a string starts with 'a', followed by any characters, and ends with 'b'.\n    Uses the regex pattern '^a.*b$'.\n    \"\"\"\n    return bool(re.fullmatch(r'a.*b', text))"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/798", "solution": "from typing import Optional\nimport numbers\n\nclass Validator:\n    def validate_iterable(self, arr) -> None:\n        try:\n            iter(arr)\n        except TypeError:\n            raise TypeError(\"arr must be an iterable of numeric values\")\n\n    def validate_numeric(self, elem, index: Optional[int] = None) -> None:\n        if not isinstance(elem, numbers.Number):\n            if index is not None:\n                raise TypeError(f\"element at position {index} is not numeric\")\n            else:\n                raise TypeError(\"element is not numeric\")\n\ndef _sum(arr):\n    \"\"\"Compute arithmetic sum of numeric elements in arr per design.\"\"\"\n    validator = Validator()\n    validator.validate_iterable(arr)\n\n    acc = 0\n    iterator = iter(arr)\n\n    for index, elem in enumerate(iterator):\n        validator.validate_numeric(elem, index)\n        acc = acc + elem\n\n    return acc"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/801", "solution": "def test_three_equal(a: int, b: int, c: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of equal numbers from three given integers.\n    Raises TypeError if any input is not strictly an int (e.g., bool is rejected).\n    \"\"\"\n    def validate_types(a_val, b_val, c_val) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Ensures all arguments are of type int, raising TypeError otherwise.\"\"\"\n        if not (type(a_val) is int and type(b_val) is int and type(c_val) is int):\n            raise TypeError(\"All arguments must be of type int\")\n\n    def count_equals(a_val, b_val, c_val) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Counts and returns 3, 2, or 0 based on equality of the three inputs.\"\"\"\n        if a_val == b_val == c_val:\n            return 3\n        elif a_val == b_val or a_val == c_val or b_val == c_val:\n            return 2\n        else:\n            return 0\n\n    validate_types(a, b, c)\n    return count_equals(a, b, c)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/800", "solution": "import re\n\ndef remove_all_spaces(s):\n    \"\"\"Remove all whitespace characters from s.\n    Parameters:\n        s (str): input string\n    Returns:\n        str: input with all characters c where c.isspace() == True removed\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: if s is not an instance of str\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(s, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"remove_all_spaces expects a str\")\n    return re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \"\", s)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/799", "solution": "def left_rotate(value: int, d: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Rotates the bits of a 32-bit unsigned integer `value` left by `d` positions.\n    Ensures 32-bit unsigned behavior by masking inputs and outputs.\n\n    Args:\n        value: The integer whose 32-bit representation will be rotated.\n        d: Number of positions to rotate left (must be a non-negative int).\n\n    Returns:\n        The result of rotating `value` left by `d` bits, as a 32-bit unsigned int (0..0xFFFFFFFF).\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: if `value` or `d` is not an int.\n        ValueError: if `d` is negative.\n\n    Example:\n        assert left_rotate(16, 2) == 64\n    \"\"\"\n    # Validate inputs: type checks and negative shift check\n    if not isinstance(value, int) or not isinstance(d, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Both 'value' and 'd' must be integers.\")\n    if d < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"'d' (shift amount) cannot be negative.\")\n\n    MASK32 = 0xFFFFFFFF  # 32-bit mask for unsigned emulation\n\n    # Mask the input value to ensure it's treated as a 32-bit unsigned integer\n    masked_value = value & MASK32\n\n    # Calculate the effective shift amount, wrapping around 32 bits\n    shift = d % 32\n\n    # If the effective shift is 0, no rotation is needed\n    if shift == 0:\n        return masked_value\n    else:\n        # Perform the left rotation: (bits shifted left) OR (bits shifted right to wrap around)\n        # The final result is masked to ensure it stays within the 32-bit unsigned range.\n        rotated = ((masked_value << shift) | (masked_value >> (32 - shift))) & MASK32\n        return rotated"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/797", "solution": "def sum_in_range(l, r):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of all odd natural numbers within the inclusive range [l, r].\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: if l or r is not an int.\n        ValueError: if l > r or if l < 1 or r < 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Input validation\n    if not isinstance(l, int) or not isinstance(r, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"l and r must be integers\")\n    if l > r:\n        raise ValueError(\"l must be <= r\")\n    if l < 1 or r < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"l and r must be natural numbers (>= 1)\")\n\n    # Number of odd numbers <= x is (x + 1) // 2, and sum of first k odd numbers = k^2.\n    count_r = (r + 1) // 2\n    sum_up_to_r = count_r * count_r\n\n    count_l_minus_1 = (l - 1 + 1) // 2  # simplifies to l // 2\n    sum_up_to_l_minus_1 = count_l_minus_1 * count_l_minus_1\n\n    return sum_up_to_r - sum_up_to_l_minus_1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/803", "solution": "def is_perfect_square(n, return_root: bool = False):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a number is a perfect square, with an option to return its integer square root.\n\n    Args:\n        n: int or float to check. Integral floats are treated as integers.\n        return_root: if True, return a tuple (is_square: bool, root: Optional[int]),\n                     otherwise return just a bool.\n\n    Returns:\n        bool or (bool, Optional[int])\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: if n is not an int or float.\n    \"\"\"\n    import math\n    from typing import Optional, Tuple, Union\n\n    def _validate(val) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Validates that the input is an integer or a float.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(val, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input must be an integer or a float.\")\n\n    def _to_int_if_integral_float(val) -> Union[int, float]:\n        \"\"\"Converts integral floats to integers; otherwise returns the value unchanged.\"\"\"\n        if isinstance(val, float) and val.is_integer():\n            return int(val)\n        return val\n\n    def _compute_isqrt(val: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Computes the integer square root using math.isqrt.\"\"\"\n        return math.isqrt(val)\n\n    _validate(n)\n\n    normalized_n = _to_int_if_integral_float(n)\n\n    if isinstance(normalized_n, float):\n        # Non-integral float, not a perfect square\n        if return_root:\n            return False, None\n        return False\n\n    if normalized_n < 0:\n        # Negative number, not a perfect square\n        if return_root:\n            return False, None\n        return False\n\n    # At this point, normalized_n is a non-negative integer\n    root_candidate = _compute_isqrt(normalized_n)\n    is_square = (root_candidate * root_candidate == normalized_n)\n\n    if return_root:\n        return is_square, root_candidate if is_square else None\n    return is_square"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/804", "solution": "def is_product_even(nums) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the product of numbers in an iterable is an even integer.\n    The product is considered 1 for an empty iterable.\n    \"\"\"\n    product = 1.0\n    found_elements = False\n\n    for num in nums:\n        found_elements = True\n        val = float(num)  # This will raise TypeError/ValueError if not convertible\n        if val == 0.0:\n            return True\n        product *= val\n\n    if not found_elements:\n        return False  # Empty product is 1, which is odd.\n\n    # Check if the product is integral within tolerance\n    if abs(round(product) - product) > 1e-9:\n        return False\n\n    # Check parity of the integral product\n    return int(round(product)) % 2 == 0"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/805", "solution": "from typing import List, Union\n\ndef max_sum_list(list_of_lists: List[List[Union[float, int]]]) -> List[Union[float, int]] | None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the inner list from a list of lists whose sum of elements is the highest.\n    If multiple lists have the same highest sum, the first one encountered is returned.\n    Returns None for an empty top-level list.\n\n    Args:\n        list_of_lists: A list of lists containing numeric values (int or float).\n\n    Returns:\n        The inner list with the largest sum, or None if list_of_lists is empty.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If list_of_lists is not a list, any inner element is not a list,\n                   or any inner element contains non-numeric values.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(list_of_lists, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"list_of_lists must be a list of lists\")\n\n    if len(list_of_lists) == 0:\n        return None\n\n    best_list = None\n    best_sum = float(\"-inf\")\n\n    for inner in list_of_lists:\n        if not isinstance(inner, list):\n            raise TypeError(\"Each element of list_of_lists must be a list\")\n        # Validate numeric elements and compute sum\n        s = 0\n        for v in inner:\n            if not isinstance(v, (int, float)):\n                raise TypeError(\"All elements of inner lists must be int or float\")\n            s += v\n\n        if s > best_sum:\n            best_sum = s\n            best_list = inner\n\n    return best_list"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/807", "solution": "def is_odd(n):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is odd based on modulo operation.\"\"\"\n    return n % 2 != 0\n\ndef first_odd(numbers):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the first odd number in a list.\n    Returns the first odd integer found, or None if no odd numbers exist.\n    \"\"\"\n    for number in numbers:\n        if is_odd(number):\n            return number\n    return None"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/806", "solution": "def max_run_uppercase(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the maximum run of uppercase characters in a string.\n    This runs in O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space, handling edge cases like empty strings or no uppercase characters.\n    \"\"\"\n    current_run = 0\n    max_run = 0\n    for char in s:\n        if char.isupper():\n            current_run += 1\n            if current_run > max_run:\n                max_run = current_run\n        else:\n            current_run = 0\n    return max_run"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/809", "solution": "def check_smaller(a: tuple, b: tuple) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if each element of tuple `b` is strictly smaller than its corresponding element in tuple `a`.\n\n    Parameters:\n    - a (tuple): First tuple containing the reference elements.\n    - b (tuple): Second tuple whose elements should be strictly smaller than those in `a`.\n\n    Returns:\n    - bool: True if for every index i, b[i] < a[i]; False if any comparison is False.\n\n    Raises:\n    - TypeError: if `a` or `b` is not a tuple, or if any pairwise comparison raises TypeError.\n    - ValueError: if `a` and `b` have different lengths.\n\n    Complexity:\n    - Time: O(n), where n = len(a) == len(b).\n    - Space: O(1) additional space.\n\n    Examples:\n    >>> check_smaller((3, 4), (1, 2))\n    True\n    >>> check_smaller((3, 4), (3, 2))\n    False\n    >>> check_smaller((), ())\n    True\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(a, tuple) or not isinstance(b, tuple):\n        raise TypeError(\"Both inputs 'a' and 'b' must be tuples.\")\n\n    if len(a) != len(b):\n        raise ValueError(\"Tuples 'a' and 'b' must have the same length.\")\n\n    for i in range(len(a)):\n        if not (b[i] < a[i]):\n            return False\n\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/808", "solution": "def check_K(t: tuple, k: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if the given tuple contains the integer k at the top level.\n\n    Args:\n        t: The tuple to inspect for membership.\n        k: The integer value to search for within the tuple.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if k is found as a top-level integer element in t, False otherwise.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If t is not a tuple or k is not an integer.\n\n    Complexity:\n        Time: O(n) where n is the length of the tuple.\n        Space: O(1) additional space.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> check_K((10, 4, 5, 6, 8), 6)\n        True\n        >>> check_K((1, 2, '3'), 3)\n        False\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _validate_args(t_arg: tuple, k_arg: int) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Validates the types of the input arguments.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(t_arg, tuple):\n            raise TypeError(\"The first argument must be a tuple.\")\n        if not isinstance(k_arg, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"The second argument must be an integer.\")\n\n    def _contains_top_level(t_arg: tuple, k_arg: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Performs the top-level membership scan.\"\"\"\n        for elem in t_arg:\n            if isinstance(elem, int) and elem == k_arg:\n                return True\n        return False\n\n    _validate_args(t, k)\n    return _contains_top_level(t, k)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/474", "solution": "class InvalidCharacterError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid character inputs.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str) -> None:\n        self.message = message\n        super().__init__(self.message)\n\nclass StringValue:\n    \"\"\"Wrapper for string values, providing length helper.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: str) -> None:\n        self.value = value\n\n    def length(self) -> int:\n        return len(self.value)\n\nclass Character:\n    \"\"\"Value object for a single character string.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, char: str) -> None:\n        self.char = char\n\n    def is_single_char(self) -> bool:\n        return isinstance(self.char, str) and len(self.char) == 1\n\nclass ReplaceOperation:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates the character replacement algorithm.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, original: StringValue, target: Character, replacement: Character) -> None:\n        self.original = original\n        self.target = target\n        self.replacement = replacement\n\n    def execute(self) -> str:\n        if not self.target.is_single_char():\n            raise InvalidCharacterError(\"Target character must be a single character string.\")\n        if not self.replacement.is_single_char():\n            raise InvalidCharacterError(\"Replacement character must be a single character string.\")\n\n        result_chars = []\n        for char in self.original.value:\n            if char == self.target.char:\n                result_chars.append(self.replacement.char)\n            else:\n                result_chars.append(char)\n        return \"\".join(result_chars)\n\ndef replace_char(original_string, target_char, replacement_char):\n    \"\"\"\n    Replaces all occurrences of a target character with a replacement character in a string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(target_char, str) or len(target_char) != 1:\n        raise InvalidCharacterError(\"Target character must be a single character string.\")\n    if not isinstance(replacement_char, str) or len(replacement_char) != 1:\n        raise InvalidCharacterError(\"Replacement character must be a single character string.\")\n\n    original_sv = StringValue(original_string)\n    target_ch = Character(target_char)\n    replacement_ch = Character(replacement_char)\n\n    operation = ReplaceOperation(original_sv, target_ch, replacement_ch)\n    return operation.execute()"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "def check_Consecutive(lst: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a list contains consecutive integers, regardless of order.\n    Returns True if elements are unique integers that form a consecutive sequence.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not lst:\n        return False\n\n    if len(lst) == 1:\n        return True\n\n    if not all(isinstance(x, int) for x in lst):\n        raise TypeError(\"All elements must be integers\")\n\n    unique_elements = set(lst)\n\n    if len(unique_elements) != len(lst):\n        return False\n\n    min_val = min(unique_elements)\n    max_val = max(unique_elements)\n\n    return (max_val - min_val) == len(lst) - 1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/470", "solution": "def add_pairwise(input_tuple: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Return a tuple of pairwise sums of neighboring elements.\n\n    Example:\n        add_pairwise((1, 5, 7, 8, 10)) -> (6, 12, 15, 18)\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(input_tuple)\n    if n < 2:\n        return ()\n    results = []\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        results.append(input_tuple[i] + input_tuple[i + 1])\n    return tuple(results)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "from typing import List, Optional, Union\n\nNumber = Union[int, float]\n\ndef max_product(arr: List[Number]) -> Optional[Number]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum product formed by multiplying numbers of an increasing subsequence of the array.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return None\n\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    # Initialize DP arrays.\n    # maxProd[i] stores the maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    # minProd[i] stores the minimum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    # Each element itself is a valid single-element increasing subsequence.\n    maxProd = list(arr)\n    minProd = list(arr)\n\n    # Fill DP arrays using the recurrence relation.\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            # Only consider strictly increasing subsequences, i.e., arr[j] must be less than arr[i].\n            if arr[j] < arr[i]:\n                current_val = arr[i]\n\n                # Calculate candidate products by extending subsequences ending at j.\n                candidate1 = current_val * maxProd[j]\n                candidate2 = current_val * minProd[j]\n\n                # Update maxProd[i] and minProd[i] with the largest/smallest product found.\n                maxProd[i] = max(maxProd[i], candidate1, candidate2)\n                minProd[i] = min(minProd[i], candidate1, candidate2)\n\n    # The final result is the maximum value found across all maxProd[i].\n    return max(maxProd)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/471", "solution": "from typing import List\n\ndef find_remainder(arr: List[int], n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the product of array elements modulo n using iterative modular multiplication.\"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be > 0\")\n\n    if n == 1:\n        return 0\n\n    prod = 1\n    for value in arr:\n        prod = (prod * (value % n)) % n\n\n    return prod"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "from collections import Counter\nfrom typing import List, Tuple, Dict, Any\n\nclass Validator:\n    \"\"\"Helper class for input validation.\"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def validate_list_of_tuples(lst: list, arg_name: str) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Raises TypeError if `lst` is not a list or contains non-tuple elements.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(lst, list):\n            raise TypeError(f\"{arg_name} must be a list of tuples\")\n        for item in lst:\n            if not isinstance(item, tuple):\n                raise TypeError(f\"{arg_name} must be a list of tuples\")\n\ndef _canonicalize(t: tuple):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a canonical key for a tuple, making it order-insensitive and multiplicity-preserving.\n    Tries sorting elements; if not comparable, uses a Counter-based key if elements are hashable.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        # Preferred: tuple(sorted(t)) if elements are comparable\n        return tuple(sorted(t))\n    except TypeError:\n        # Fallback: if elements not comparable, try Counter if elements are hashable\n        try:\n            counts = Counter(t)\n            # Create a stable key from sorted (element, count) pairs\n            return tuple(sorted(counts.items()))\n        except TypeError as e:\n            # If elements are neither comparable nor hashable, raise an error\n            element_types = \", \".join(sorted(str(type(elem)) for elem in t))\n            raise TypeError(f\"tuple elements are not comparable or hashable: {{{element_types}}}\") from e\n\ndef _count_canonical_map(lst: list) -> Dict[Any, List[Tuple]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Builds a map from canonical tuple keys to a list of their original tuple representatives.\n    This helps in selecting a consistent representative for the intersection.\n    \"\"\"\n    canonical_map = {}\n    for original_tuple in lst:\n        canonical_key = _canonicalize(original_tuple)\n        if canonical_key not in canonical_map:\n            canonical_map[canonical_key] = []\n        canonical_map[canonical_key].append(original_tuple)\n    return canonical_map\n\ndef tuple_intersection(list1, list2) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the tuple intersection of elements in the given tuple list irrespective of their order.\n    Returns a new list containing one representative tuple for each canonical match, in deterministic order.\n    \"\"\"\n    Validator.validate_list_of_tuples(list1, \"list1\")\n    Validator.validate_list_of_tuples(list2, \"list2\")\n\n    map1 = _count_canonical_map(list1)\n    map2 = _count_canonical_map(list2)\n\n    # Compute common canonical keys and sort them for deterministic output\n    common_keys = sorted(map1.keys() & map2.keys())\n\n    result = []\n    for key in common_keys:\n        # Pick one representative tuple from the first list's map for the result\n        result.append(map1[key][0])\n\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/475", "solution": "from typing import Any, List, Tuple\n\ndef _validate_input(d: Any) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Validates if the input 'd' is a dictionary.\n    Raises TypeError if 'd' is not a dict.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(d, dict):\n        raise TypeError(\"d must be a dict\")\n\ndef _extract_items(d: dict) -> List[Tuple[Any, Any]]:\n    \"\"\"Extracts items from the dictionary as a list of (key, value) tuples.\n    This creates a shallow copy, preserving the original dictionary.\"\"\"\n    return list(d.items())\n\ndef _sort_items(items: List[Tuple[Any, Any]]) -> List[Tuple[Any, Any]]:\n    \"\"\"Sorts a list of (key, value) tuples by value in descending order.\n    Raises TypeError if values are not comparable.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        return sorted(items, key=lambda kv: kv[1], reverse=True)\n    except TypeError:\n        raise TypeError(\"values not comparable\")\n\ndef sort_counter(d: dict) -> List[Tuple[Any, Any]]:\n    \"\"\"Sorts a dictionary by its values in descending order.\n\n    This function takes a dictionary and returns a new list of its (key, value)\n    items, sorted in descending order by value. It performs input validation\n    and handles non-comparable values.\n\n    Args:\n        d: The input dictionary. Values must be mutually comparable for sorting.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list of (key, value) tuples sorted by value (descending).\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If 'd' is not a dictionary or if values are not comparable.\n\n    Complexity:\n        Time: O(n log n) (due to sorting).\n        Space: O(n) (for storing items).\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> sort_counter({'Math': 81, 'Physics': 83, 'Chemistry': 87})\n        [('Chemistry', 87), ('Physics', 83), ('Math', 81)]\n        >>> sort_counter({'a': 5, 'b': 1, 'c': 3})\n        [('a', 5), ('c', 3), ('b', 1)]\n        >>> sort_counter({})\n        []\n        >>> sort_counter({'x': 2, 'y': 2})\n        [('x', 2), ('y', 2)] # Stable sort preserves original order for ties.\n        >>> sort_counter(None) # doctest: +ELLIPSIS\n        Traceback (most recent call last):\n            ...\n        TypeError: d must be a dict\n        >>> sort_counter({'a': 1, 'b': 'string'}) # doctest: +ELLIPSIS\n        Traceback (most recent call last):\n            ...\n        TypeError: values not comparable\n    \"\"\"\n    _validate_input(d)\n    items = _extract_items(d)\n    return _sort_items(items)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/463", "solution": "from typing import Union\n\ndef _validate_non_empty(arr) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Raises ValueError if the input array is empty.\"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input array cannot be empty.\")\n\ndef _validate_elements_numeric(arr) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Raises TypeError if any element in the array is not an int or float.\"\"\"\n    for element in arr:\n        if not isinstance(element, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements in the array must be numeric (int or float).\")\n\nclass ComputationState:\n    \"\"\"Manages the running maximum, minimum, and overall maximum product.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, initial: Union[int, float]) -> None:\n        self.current_max: Union[int, float] = initial\n        self.current_min: Union[int, float] = initial\n        self.global_max: Union[int, float] = initial\n\n    def update(self, value: Union[int, float]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Updates the state based on a new value, handling zeros and negatives.\"\"\"\n        if value == 0:\n            self.current_max = 0\n            self.current_min = 0\n            self.global_max = max(self.global_max, 0)\n            return\n\n        # To calculate new min, we need the old current_max, so store it temporarily.\n        temp_current_max = self.current_max\n\n        self.current_max = max(value, temp_current_max * value, self.current_min * value)\n        self.current_min = min(value, temp_current_max * value, self.current_min * value)\n\n        self.global_max = max(self.global_max, self.current_max)\n\n    def get_global(self) -> Union[int, float]:\n        \"\"\"Returns the overall maximum product found so far.\"\"\"\n        return self.global_max\n\ndef max_subarray_product(arr):\n    \"\"\"Compute maximum product of any contiguous non-empty subarray.\n    Raises ValueError if arr is empty, TypeError if any element is not int or float.\n    \"\"\"\n    _validate_non_empty(arr)\n    _validate_elements_numeric(arr)\n\n    state = ComputationState(arr[0])\n    for i in range(1, len(arr)):\n        state.update(arr[i])\n\n    return state.get_global()"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/476", "solution": "def big_sum(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the sum of the largest and smallest value in a given array (iterable).\n    Raises ValueError if the input array is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    it = iter(arr)\n    try:\n        min_val = max_val = next(it)\n    except StopIteration:\n        raise ValueError(\"arr is empty\")\n\n    for x in it:\n        if x < min_val:\n            min_val = x\n        if x > max_val:\n            max_val = x\n\n    return min_val + max_val"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/477", "solution": "def is_lower(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Converts the given string to its Unicode-lowercased form.\n\n    Raises TypeError if the input is not a string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(s, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"is_lower: input must be of type 'str'\")\n    return s.lower()"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/478", "solution": "def remove_lowercase(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Removes all lowercase characters, effectively removing lowercase substrings.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(s, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"remove_lowercase() expects a str\")\n    if s == \"\":\n        return \"\"\n\n    result_chars: list[str] = []\n    for c in s:\n        if not c.islower():\n            result_chars.append(c)\n    return ''.join(result_chars)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/555", "solution": "def validate_type(n) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Validates that n is an integer.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be int\")\n\ndef validate_non_negative(n: int) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Validates that n is non-negative.\"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be non-negative\")\n\ndef compute(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Computes the difference using closed-form formulas: t = n(n+1)/2, result = t*(t-1).\"\"\"\n    t = n * (n + 1) // 2\n    result = t * (t - 1)\n    return result\n\ndef difference(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the difference between the sum of cubes of the first n natural numbers\n    and the sum of the first n natural numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    validate_type(n)\n    validate_non_negative(n)\n    return compute(n)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/554", "solution": "from typing import List\n\ndef Split(nums: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Validate input and return a new list containing only odd integers from nums.\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: if nums is not a list\n        ValueError: if any element in nums is not an int\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(nums, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Split expects a list of integers as input\")\n    result: List[int] = []\n    for x in nums:\n        if not isinstance(x, int):\n            raise ValueError(\"All elements must be int\")\n        if x % 2 != 0:\n            result.append(x)\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/556", "solution": "def countParity(arr_local, m_local):\n    \"\"\"Helper to count even and odd integers among the first m_local elements of arr_local.\n    Raises TypeError if an element is not an integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    even_count_local = 0\n    odd_count_local = 0\n\n    for i in range(m_local):\n        x = arr_local[i]\n\n        if not isinstance(x, int):\n            raise TypeError(f\"Elements in array must be integers, got {type(x).__name__}\")\n\n        if x % 2 == 0:\n            even_count_local += 1\n        else:\n            odd_count_local += 1\n\n    return even_count_local, odd_count_local\n\ndef find_Odd_Pair(arr, n):\n    \"\"\"Counts pairs in arr whose XOR value is odd.\n    Considers the first min(n, len(arr)) elements and returns even_count * odd_count.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an integer.\")\n\n    try:\n        arr_len = len(arr)\n    except TypeError:\n        raise TypeError(\"arr must be an iterable that supports len(), e.g., a list or tuple of integers.\")\n\n    m = min(n, arr_len)\n\n    if m < 2:\n        return 0\n\n    even_count, odd_count = countParity(arr, m)\n\n    return even_count * odd_count"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/557", "solution": "def toggle_string(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Toggles the case of all alphabetic characters in a string, preserving non-alphabetic characters.\"\"\"\n    return \"\".join(char.swapcase() for char in s)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/558", "solution": "def to_digit_string(n: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Converts a non-negative integer to its string representation.\"\"\"\n    return str(n)\n\ndef pad_digits(s1: str, s2: str) -> tuple[str, str]:\n    \"\"\"Left-pads the shorter string with '0' to match the length of the longer string.\"\"\"\n    len1, len2 = len(s1), len(s2)\n    max_len = max(len1, len2)\n    padded_s1 = s1.zfill(max_len)\n    padded_s2 = s2.zfill(max_len)\n    return padded_s1, padded_s2\n\ndef compare_and_sum(s1: str, s2: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Computes the sum of absolute differences between corresponding digits of two equal-length strings.\"\"\"\n    total_diff = 0\n    for i in range(len(s1)):\n        total_diff += abs(int(s1[i]) - int(s2[i]))\n    return total_diff\n\ndef digit_distance_nums(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of per-digit absolute differences between two integers,\n    using their absolute values and left-padding the shorter digit string with zeros.\n    This function ensures O(n) time and space complexity, where n is the number of digits\n    in the larger input, handling negative numbers, zeros, and different lengths.\n    \"\"\"\n    abs_a, abs_b = abs(a), abs(b)\n\n    s_a = to_digit_string(abs_a)\n    s_b = to_digit_string(abs_b)\n\n    p_s_a, p_s_b = pad_digits(s_a, s_b)\n\n    total = compare_and_sum(p_s_a, p_s_b)\n\n    return total"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/479", "solution": "def first_Digit(value):\n    \"\"\"\n    Return the most significant decimal digit of the absolute value of `value`.\n    - For |value| >= 1: returns the leftmost digit of the integer part.\n    - For 0 < |value| < 1: returns the first non-zero digit in the fractional part.\n    - For 0 (including -0.0): returns 0.\n    Raises TypeError for non-numeric inputs and ValueError for NaN/Inf or if the digit\n    cannot be determined due to floating-point precision limits.\n    \"\"\"\n    import math\n\n    if not isinstance(value, (int, float)):\n        raise TypeError(\"value must be an int or float\")\n    if not math.isfinite(value):\n        raise ValueError(\"value must be a finite number\")\n\n    v = abs(value)\n    if v == 0.0:\n        return 0\n\n    if v >= 1.0:\n        # Reduce by powers of 10 until the leading digit is the integer part.\n        while v >= 10.0:\n            v /= 10.0\n        return int(v)\n    else:\n        # 0 < v < 1.0: multiply until first non-zero digit appears\n        max_iterations = 308  # conservative cap for IEEE-754 double precision\n        for _ in range(max_iterations):\n            v *= 10.0\n            digit = int(v)\n            if digit != 0:\n                return digit\n        raise ValueError(\"Unable to determine leading digit due to precision limits\")"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/560", "solution": "class TupleInput:\n    \"\"\"Wraps an input tuple for read-only access.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, elements: tuple) -> None:\n        self.elements = elements\n\nclass ElementSet:\n    \"\"\"Maintains a unique set of elements and can convert them to a sorted tuple.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._elements = set()\n\n    def add(self, element) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Adds an element to the internal set, ensuring uniqueness.\"\"\"\n        self._elements.add(element)\n\n    def to_sorted_tuple(self) -> tuple:\n        \"\"\"Converts the unique elements to a sorted tuple.\"\"\"\n        return tuple(sorted(list(self._elements)))\n\nclass UnionResult:\n    \"\"\"Holds the final sorted tuple result of the union operation.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, elements: tuple) -> None:\n        self.elements = elements\n\nclass UnionProcessor:\n    \"\"\"Orchestrates the process of finding the sorted union of elements from two tuples.\"\"\"\n    def union_elements(self, tuple_a: tuple, tuple_b: tuple) -> tuple:\n        input_a = TupleInput(tuple_a)\n        input_b = TupleInput(tuple_b)\n\n        element_set = ElementSet()\n\n        for element in input_a.elements:\n            element_set.add(element)\n\n        for element in input_b.elements:\n            element_set.add(element)\n\n        sorted_union_tuple = element_set.to_sorted_tuple()\n\n        union_result = UnionResult(sorted_union_tuple)\n        return union_result.elements\n\ndef union_elements(tuple_a: tuple, tuple_b: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the union of elements from two tuples, deduplicates them, sorts them,\n    and returns the result as a new tuple.\n    \"\"\"\n    processor = UnionProcessor()\n    return processor.union_elements(tuple_a, tuple_b)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/559", "solution": "def max_sub_array_sum(arr, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the sum of the largest contiguous sublist within the first n elements of arr\n    using Kadane's algorithm. Raises ValueError or TypeError for invalid inputs.\n    \"\"\"\n    if arr is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"arr is None\")\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be a positive integer\")\n\n    processed_count = min(n, len(arr))\n\n    if processed_count == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"no elements to process\")\n\n    # Initialize current_sum and max_sum with the first processed element\n    first_element = arr[0]\n    if not isinstance(first_element, (int, float)):\n        raise TypeError(\"non-numeric element encountered\")\n\n    current_sum = first_element\n    max_sum = first_element\n\n    # Iterate through the remaining elements (from the second processed element)\n    for i in range(1, processed_count):\n        element = arr[i]\n        if not isinstance(element, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(\"non-numeric element encountered\")\n\n        current_sum = max(element, current_sum + element)\n        max_sum = max(max_sum, current_sum)\n\n    return max_sum"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/562", "solution": "def Find_Max_Length(lst: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the length of the longest immediate sublist. Returns 0 for an empty input list.\n\n    Note: This implementation assumes `lst` is a list of sequences and does not perform\n    explicit input validation to raise TypeError for non-list inputs or non-list elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    max_len = 0\n    for sub in lst:\n        max_len = max(max_len, len(sub))\n    return max_len"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/563", "solution": "import re\nimport ast\nimport warnings\nfrom typing import List\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n# Regex pattern to match either double-quoted or single-quoted string literals.\n# It handles escaped quotes and backslashes within the literal.\n_QUOTE_PATTERN = r'(\\\"([^\\\"\\\\]|\\\\.)*\\\")|(\\'([^\\'\\\\]|\\\\.)*\\')'\n\n@dataclass\nclass QuotedSubstring:\n    \"\"\"Represents a matched quoted fragment with its raw text and positional information.\"\"\"\n    raw_text: str\n    start_index: int\n    end_index: int\n    quote_char: str\n\nclass LiteralUnescaper:\n    \"\"\"Safely unescapes Python string literals using ast.literal_eval.\"\"\"\n    def unescape(self, raw_text: str) -> str:\n        return ast.literal_eval(raw_text)\n\nclass RegexMatcher:\n    \"\"\"Locates quoted substrings in a string using a regular expression.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, pattern: str):\n        self._compiled_pattern = re.compile(pattern)\n        # Heuristic threshold for detecting potentially pathological regex inputs.\n        self._pathological_threshold = 1000\n\n    def findall(self, raw: str) -> List[QuotedSubstring]:\n        # If a long sequence of backslashes is present, use a linear scan to avoid regex backtracking.\n        if '\\\\' * self._pathological_threshold in raw:\n            warnings.warn(\n                f\"Pathological input heuristic triggered. Falling back to linear scan.\"\n            )\n            return self._linear_scan_fallback(raw)\n\n        matches = []\n        for m in self._compiled_pattern.finditer(raw):\n            raw_text = m.group(0)\n            start_index = m.start()\n            end_index = m.end()\n            quote_char = raw_text[0]\n            matches.append(QuotedSubstring(raw_text, start_index, end_index, quote_char))\n        return matches\n\n    def _linear_scan_fallback(self, raw: str) -> List[QuotedSubstring]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Linearly scans the input string to extract quoted substrings.\n        This method is a fallback to avoid regex backtracking issues on crafted inputs.\n        \"\"\"\n        extracted_fragments = []\n        i = 0\n        n = len(raw)\n\n        while i < n:\n            if raw[i] == '\"' or raw[i] == \"'\":\n                quote_char = raw[i]\n                start_index = i\n                i += 1  # Move past opening quote\n\n                content_buffer = []  # Stores chars between quotes, including escape sequences\n                found_closing_quote = False\n                while i < n:\n                    if raw[i] == '\\\\':\n                        content_buffer.append(raw[i])  # Append backslash\n                        i += 1  # Move to next char, which is escaped\n                        if i < n:\n                            content_buffer.append(raw[i])  # Append escaped char\n                        else:\n                            warnings.warn(f\"Skipping malformed literal ending with escape at index {start_index}\")\n                            break  # String ends with an unescaped backslash, malformed\n                    elif raw[i] == quote_char:\n                        found_closing_quote = True\n                        break  # Found closing quote\n                    else:\n                        content_buffer.append(raw[i])\n                    i += 1\n\n                if found_closing_quote:\n                    end_index = i + 1  # Include closing quote in end_index\n                    raw_text = quote_char + \"\".join(content_buffer) + quote_char\n                    extracted_fragments.append(QuotedSubstring(raw_text, start_index, end_index, quote_char))\n                    i += 1  # Move past closing quote\n                else:\n                    warnings.warn(f\"Skipping unterminated quoted literal starting at index {start_index}\")\n            else:\n                i += 1  # Move to next character if not a quote\n        return extracted_fragments\n\n\nclass ParserEngine:\n    \"\"\"Orchestrates the extraction, unescaping, and error handling of quoted substrings.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, pattern: str):\n        self._regex_matcher = RegexMatcher(pattern)\n        self._literal_unescaper = LiteralUnescaper()\n\n    def extract(self, raw: str) -> List[str]:\n        # Raises TypeError if input is not a string, then extracts and unescapes values.\n        if not isinstance(raw, str):\n            raise TypeError(\"input_str must be a str\")\n\n        extracted_values = []\n        quoted_substrings = self._regex_matcher.findall(raw)\n\n        for qs in quoted_substrings:\n            try:\n                # ast.literal_eval expects the full literal, including the surrounding quotes.\n                unescaped_content = self._literal_unescaper.unescape(qs.raw_text)\n                extracted_values.append(unescaped_content)\n            except (ValueError, SyntaxError) as e:\n                # Malformed or invalid literals are skipped, and a warning is issued.\n                warnings.warn(\n                    f\"Skipping invalid quoted literal at indices {qs.start_index}-{qs.end_index} \"\n                    f\"('{qs.raw_text[:20]}...') due to unescaping error: {e}\"\n                )\n        return extracted_values\n\ndef extract_values(input_str: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Extracts and unescapes values enclosed in single or double quotation marks from a string.\n    Malformed or unterminated quoted fragments are skipped with a warning.\n    \"\"\"\n    parser = ParserEngine(_QUOTE_PATTERN)\n    return parser.extract(input_str)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/564", "solution": "from typing import List\n\ndef count_Pairs(arr: List[int], length: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of unordered pairs (i < j) among arr[0:length] where arr[i] != arr[j].\n    Uses the formula total_pairs - equal_pairs for O(n) efficiency.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(length, int) or length < 0 or length > len(arr):\n        raise ValueError(\"length must be an int between 0 and len(arr)\")\n\n    if length < 2:\n        return 0\n\n    # Validate elements are ints and build frequency map for elements in arr[0:length]\n    freq = {}\n    for i in range(length):\n        value = arr[i]\n        if not isinstance(value, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"all elements in the considered slice must be int\")\n        freq[value] = freq.get(value, 0) + 1\n\n    # Calculate total possible pairs among 'length' elements\n    total_pairs = length * (length - 1) // 2\n\n    # Calculate pairs where elements are equal\n    equal_pairs = 0\n    for count in freq.values():\n        equal_pairs += count * (count - 1) // 2\n\n    # Unequal pairs are total pairs minus equal pairs\n    unequal_pairs = total_pairs - equal_pairs\n\n    return unequal_pairs"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/565", "solution": "def split(input_string: str) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Splits a string into a list of its individual characters, preserving order.\n    Raises TypeError if input_string is not a string.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(input_string, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"input_string must be a str\")\n    return list(input_string)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/567", "solution": "def issort_list(lst):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a list is sorted in non-decreasing order.\n    Returns True for empty or single-element lists, and propagates comparison errors.\"\"\"\n    if len(lst) <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    for i in range(len(lst) - 1):\n        if not (lst[i] <= lst[i+1]):\n            return False\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/566", "solution": "def sum_digits(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of the digits of a non-negative integer using an iterative numeric approach.\"\"\"\n    def _iterative_sum(num: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Computes the sum of digits iteratively without string conversion.\"\"\"\n        if num == 0:\n            return 0\n        digit_sum = 0\n        current_num = num\n        while current_num > 0:\n            digit_sum += current_num % 10\n            current_num //= 10\n        return digit_sum\n\n    def _string_sum(num: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Computes the sum of digits by converting the number to a string (documented alternative).\"\"\"\n        return sum(int(digit) for digit in str(num))\n\n    return _iterative_sum(n)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/568", "solution": "def empty_list(N):\n    \"\"\"\n    Creates a list containing N distinct empty dictionary objects.\n    Each dictionary is a new, unique instance.\n\n    Parameters:\n    - N (int): Number of empty dictionaries to create. Must be a non-negative integer.\n\n    Returns:\n    - list: A list of N empty dicts.\n\n    Raises:\n    - TypeError: If N is not an int.\n    - ValueError: If N is negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(N, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"N must be an int\")\n    if N < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"N must be non-negative\")\n    return [{} for _ in range(N)]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/569", "solution": "def sort_sublists(input_list: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts each sublist of strings in a given list of lists in ascending lexicographic order.\n\n    This function returns a new outer list; the input list and its sublists are not modified.\n    Algorithm: Validate input, then iterate through sublists, validate each item is a string,\n    sort the sublist, and append the sorted copy to a new result list.\n    Time complexity: O(sum_i k_i log k_i) where k_i is the length of sublist i.\n    Space complexity: O(T) where T is the total number of strings in all sublists.\n    Example:\n    sort_sublists([['b','a'], ['c']]) == [['a','b'], ['c']]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(input_list, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list of sublists\")\n\n    result_outer = []\n\n    for i, sublist in enumerate(input_list):\n        if not isinstance(sublist, list):\n            raise TypeError(f\"Element at index {i} of outer list is not a list\")\n\n        for item in sublist:\n            if not isinstance(item, str):\n                raise TypeError(f\"Sublist contains non-string item: {repr(item)}\")\n\n        sorted_copy = sorted(sublist)\n        result_outer.append(sorted_copy)\n\n    return result_outer"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/573", "solution": "def _validate_element(x):\n    \"\"\"Validates if x is an int or float, explicitly excluding bool.\n    Raises TypeError if x is not a valid numeric type.\n    \"\"\"\n    if isinstance(x, bool):\n        raise TypeError(f\"Invalid element {repr(x)} of type bool; bool is not accepted\")\n    if not isinstance(x, (int, float)):\n        raise TypeError(f\"Invalid element {repr(x)} of type {type(x).__name__}; expected int or float and not bool\")\n\ndef unique_product(lst):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the product of unique numeric elements in a list.\n    Raises TypeError if any unique element is not int/float or is bool.\n    \"\"\"\n    unique_elements = set(lst)\n    product = 1\n\n    for x in unique_elements:\n        _validate_element(x)\n        product *= x\n\n    return product"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/578", "solution": "from typing import Sequence, Any, List\n\ndef interleave_lists(a: Sequence[Any], b: Sequence[Any], c: Sequence[Any]) -> List[Any]:\n    \"\"\"Interleave three sequences of equal length into a new flat list.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: if any argument is not iterable or does not support len().\n        ValueError: if input lengths differ.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        iter(a)\n        iter(b)\n        iter(c)\n    except TypeError as exc:\n        raise TypeError('one or more arguments are not iterable') from exc\n\n    try:\n        n_a, n_b, n_c = len(a), len(b), len(c)\n    except TypeError as exc:\n        raise TypeError('one or more arguments do not support len()') from exc\n\n    if not (n_a == n_b == n_c):\n        raise ValueError(f'input lengths differ: len(a)={n_a}, len(b)={n_b}, len(c)={n_c}')\n\n    return [x for trio in zip(a, b, c) for x in trio]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/576", "solution": "def is_Sub_Array(main, sub):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a list 'sub' is a contiguous sublist of 'main' using the KMP algorithm.\n    It validates inputs and handles early exit conditions.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(main, list) or not isinstance(sub, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Both 'main' and 'sub' must be lists.\")\n\n    if not sub:  # Handles the case where 'sub' is an empty list\n        return True\n    if len(sub) > len(main):  # 'sub' cannot be a sublist if it's longer\n        return False\n\n    def _build_prefix_array(pattern_list):\n        \"\"\"Computes the Longest Proper Prefix Suffix (LPS) array for KMP algorithm.\"\"\"\n        m = len(pattern_list)\n        lps = [0] * m\n        length = 0  # Length of the previous longest prefix suffix\n        i = 1\n        while i < m:\n            if pattern_list[i] == pattern_list[length]:\n                length += 1\n                lps[i] = length\n                i += 1\n            else:\n                if length != 0:\n                    length = lps[length - 1]\n                else:\n                    lps[i] = 0\n                    i += 1\n        return lps\n\n    lps = _build_prefix_array(sub)\n\n    n = len(main)\n    m = len(sub)\n\n    i = 0  # Pointer for main list\n    j = 0  # Pointer for sub list\n\n    while i < n:\n        if main[i] == sub[j]:\n            i += 1\n            j += 1\n\n        if j == m:\n            return True  # A match is found\n\n        # Mismatch after j matches: backtrack j using LPS array\n        elif i < n and main[i] != sub[j]:\n            if j != 0:\n                j = lps[j - 1]\n            else:\n                i += 1\n\n    return False  # No sublist found"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/587", "solution": "def list_tuple(lst: list) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Convert a Python list to a tuple (shallow conversion).\n\n    Parameters:\n        lst: list - the input list to convert (must be a Python list)\n\n    Returns:\n        tuple - a tuple containing the same elements in the same order\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(lst, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"list_tuple expects a list as input\")\n    return tuple(lst)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/579", "solution": "from typing import Tuple, TypeVar\n\nT = TypeVar(\"T\")\n\ndef _validate_inputs(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Validates that both inputs are tuples, raising TypeError if not.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(a, tuple) or not isinstance(b, tuple):\n        raise TypeError(\"find_dissimilar: both arguments must be tuples\")\n\ndef _compute_using_hashsets(a: Tuple[T, ...], b: Tuple[T, ...]) -> Tuple[T, ...]:\n    \"\"\"Computes dissimilar elements using hash sets for O(N+M) average time.\"\"\"\n    s_b = set(b)\n    s_a = set(a)\n    result_list = []\n    for e in a:\n        if e not in s_b:\n            result_list.append(e)\n    for e in b:\n        if e not in s_a:\n            result_list.append(e)\n    return tuple(result_list)\n\ndef _compute_using_equality(a: Tuple[T, ...], b: Tuple[T, ...]) -> Tuple[T, ...]:\n    \"\"\"Computes dissimilar elements using linear scans for O(N*M) worst-case time.\"\"\"\n    result_list = []\n    for e in a:\n        if not any(e == x for x in b):\n            result_list.append(e)\n    for e in b:\n        if not any(e == x for x in a):\n            result_list.append(e)\n    return tuple(result_list)\n\ndef find_dissimilar(a: Tuple[T, ...], b: Tuple[T, ...]) -> Tuple[T, ...]:\n    \"\"\"Finds elements present in exactly one of two input tuples, preserving order and duplicates.\n\n    This function computes the ordered symmetric difference of two tuples. It includes elements\n    from the first tuple 'a' that are not present in 'b' (in 'a''s original order), followed by\n    elements from the second tuple 'b' that are not present in 'a' (in 'b''s original order).\n    Membership is determined by equality (==). Duplicate occurrences are preserved when unique\n    to one tuple.\n\n    Args:\n        a: The first input tuple.\n        b: The second input tuple.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new tuple containing the dissimilar elements.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If either 'a' or 'b' is not a tuple.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> find_dissimilar((3, 4, 5, 6), (5, 7, 4, 10))\n        (3, 6, 7, 10)\n        >>> find_dissimilar((1, 2, 2, 3), (2, 4))\n        (1, 2, 3, 4)\n        >>> find_dissimilar((), (1, 2))\n        (1, 2)\n        >>> find_dissimilar(([1], [2]), ([2], [3])) # Unhashable elements\n        ([1], [3])\n        >>> find_dissimilar((1, 2, 3), (1, 2, 3))\n        ()\n        >>> find_dissimilar((1, 2), ())\n        (1, 2)\n\n    Edge Cases:\n        - Non-tuple inputs: Raises TypeError.\n        - Empty tuples: If both are empty, returns an empty tuple. If one is empty,\n          all elements of the non-empty tuple are returned.\n        - Duplicates: Preserved if the element is unique to its source tuple.\n        - Equality vs. Identity: Membership is determined by `==`, not `is`.\n\n    Complexity:\n        - Hashable elements (average-case): O(N + M) time, O(N + M) additional space,\n          where N is len(a) and M is len(b).\n        - Unhashable elements (worst-case): O(N * M) time, O(1) additional space.\n    \"\"\"\n    _validate_inputs(a, b)\n\n    if not a and not b:\n        return ()\n\n    try:\n        return _compute_using_hashsets(a, b)\n    except TypeError:\n        return _compute_using_equality(a, b)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/580", "solution": "from typing import Tuple, Any\n\ndef extract_even(input: Tuple[Any, ...]) -> Tuple[Any, ...]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Removes odd integers and empty nested tuples from a nested tuple structure.\n    Raises TypeError if the top-level input is not a tuple.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(input, tuple):\n        raise TypeError(\"Top-level input must be a tuple\")\n    return process_tuple(input)\n\ndef process_tuple(t: Tuple[Any, ...]) -> Tuple[Any, ...]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Recursively filters elements within a tuple, retaining even integers and non-empty nested tuples.\n    This helper function assumes its input `t` is always a tuple.\n    \"\"\"\n    buffer = []\n    for elem in t:\n        if isinstance(elem, tuple):\n            nested = process_tuple(elem)\n            if len(nested) > 0:\n                buffer.append(nested)\n        elif isinstance(elem, int):\n            if elem % 2 == 0:\n                buffer.append(elem)\n        # Other types are implicitly omitted.\n    return tuple(buffer)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/583", "solution": "from math import comb\n\ndef _validate_input(n: int) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Validates if the input n is a non-negative integer.\n    Raises TypeError if n is not an int, or ValueError if n is negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an int\")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be >= 0\")\n\ndef _catalan_via_binomial(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Computes the nth Catalan number using the direct binomial coefficient formula.\n    It relies on math.comb for the binomial coefficient and integer division.\n    \"\"\"\n    return comb(2 * n, n) // (n + 1)\n\ndef catalan_number(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Returns the nth Catalan number using the direct binomial formula.\n    It validates the input 'n' before computing the Catalan number.\n    \"\"\"\n    _validate_input(n)\n    return _catalan_via_binomial(n)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/581", "solution": "def surface_Area(base_edge, height):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the surface area of a square pyramid, where `height` is interpreted\n    as the slant height, using the formula: base_area + lateral_area\n    = a^2 + 2*a*h (since lateral_area = 4 * (0.5 * a * h) = 2*a*h).\n\n    Example:\n        assert surface_Area(3, 4) == 33\n    \"\"\"\n    a = float(base_edge)\n    h = float(height)\n    return a * a + 2.0 * a * h"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/585", "solution": "import heapq\nfrom typing import Iterable, List, Tuple, Any\n\ndef expensive_items(items: Iterable[dict], n: int) -> List[dict]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the n most expensive items in a dataset, ordered descending by price with stable tie-breaking.\n    Uses heapq.nlargest for efficiency, falling back to sort when n >= len(items).\n\n    Parameters:\n    - items: Iterable[dict] where each dict must contain a numeric 'price' (int or float).\n    - n: int, number of top-priced items to return (must be > 0).\n\n    Returns:\n    - List[dict]: list of the n most expensive item dicts, highest price first. Items with equal price\n      preserve their original relative order (stable tie-breaking).\n\n    Raises:\n    - TypeError: if items is not iterable, if any element is not a dict, if any dict lacks a numeric 'price',\n                 or if n is not an int.\n    - ValueError: if n <= 0.\n\n    Complexity:\n    - Preferred heapq.nlargest path: O(N log k) time, O(k) extra memory.\n    - Sorted fallback: O(N log N) time, O(N) extra memory.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _is_numeric_price(value: Any) -> bool:\n        return isinstance(value, (int, float))\n\n    # Validate 'n' type and value\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an int\")\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be > 0\")\n\n    enumerated_items = []\n    try:\n        # Attempt to iterate 'items'. If 'items' itself is not iterable, enumerate will raise TypeError.\n        for i, item in enumerate(items):\n            if not isinstance(item, dict):\n                raise TypeError(\"each item must be a dict\")\n            if 'price' not in item or not _is_numeric_price(item['price']):\n                raise TypeError(\"each item must have a numeric 'price' (int or float)\")\n            enumerated_items.append((i, item))\n    except TypeError as e:\n        # If the TypeError originated from `enumerate(items)` (e.g., items is not an iterable),\n        # replace it with the specified error message. Otherwise, re-raise the original error.\n        # Note: different Python implementations produce different messages; provide a clear message.\n        if not enumerated_items:\n            raise TypeError(\"items must be an iterable of dicts\") from e\n        raise\n\n    # If the input list is empty after validation, return an empty list.\n    if not enumerated_items:\n        return []\n\n    # Define the key function for sorting and nlargest.\n    # It returns a tuple (price, -index) to prioritize higher price,\n    # and then smaller original index for stable tie-breaking on equal prices.\n    key_func = lambda pair: (pair[1]['price'], -pair[0])\n\n    selected_enumerated: List[Tuple[int, dict]]\n\n    # If 'n' is greater than or equal to the total number of items,\n    # simply sort all items. This handles the case where all items are requested.\n    if n >= len(enumerated_items):\n        selected_enumerated = sorted(enumerated_items, key=key_func, reverse=True)\n    else:\n        # Use heapq.nlargest for efficiency when 'n' is significantly smaller than the total items.\n        # heapq.nlargest itself does not guarantee the order of returned elements.\n        selected_enumerated = heapq.nlargest(n, enumerated_items, key=key_func)\n        # Re-sort the selected items to ensure descending price and stable tie-breaking\n        # in the final output order.\n        selected_enumerated.sort(key=key_func, reverse=True)\n\n    # Extract the original dictionaries from the enumerated tuples\n    # and return them as a new list of references.\n    return [item for _, item in selected_enumerated]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/588", "solution": "def big_diff(values):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the difference between the largest and smallest value in a list.\n    Returns an int if all inputs are ints and the result is integral, otherwise returns a float.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not values:\n        raise ValueError(\"insufficient values: list is empty\")\n\n    # Validate numeric elements\n    for v in values:\n        if not isinstance(v, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(\"non-numeric element detected\")\n\n    max_val = max(values)\n    min_val = min(values)\n    raw_result = max_val - min_val\n\n    # Check if all elements in the input list are integers.\n    all_inputs_are_ints = all(isinstance(x, int) for x in values)\n\n    # Check if the raw result is an integral number (e.g., 3.0 should be treated as 3).\n    is_result_integral = (raw_result == int(raw_result))\n\n    if all_inputs_are_ints and is_result_integral:\n        return int(raw_result)\n    else:\n        return float(raw_result)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/591", "solution": "def swap_List(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Interchanges the first and last elements in a list.\n    Returns a new list without modifying the input list.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(lst, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"swap_List requires a list\")\n\n    n = len(lst)\n    if n == 0:\n        return []\n    elif n == 1:\n        return [lst[0]]\n    else:\n        # Create a new list with the last element first,\n        # followed by middle elements, and then the first element.\n        return [lst[-1]] + lst[1:-1] + [lst[0]]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/593", "solution": "def removezero_ip(ip_str: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Removes leading zeros from each octet of an IPv4 address string.\n    Raises ValueError for invalid IP formats or octet values.\n    \"\"\"\n    parts = ip_str.split('.')\n    normalized_octets = []\n\n    if len(parts) != 4:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Invalid IP format: expected 4 octets, got {len(parts)}\")\n\n    for i, part in enumerate(parts):\n        pos = i + 1  # 1-indexed position for error messages\n\n        if not part:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Invalid IP format: empty octet found at position {pos}\")\n\n        if not part.isdigit():\n            raise ValueError(f\"Invalid octet '{part}' at position {pos}: contains non-digit characters\")\n\n        value = int(part)\n\n        if not (0 <= value <= 255):\n            raise ValueError(f\"Invalid octet value {value} at position {pos}: must be between 0 and 255\")\n\n        normalized_octets.append(str(value))\n\n    return \".\".join(normalized_octets)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/592", "solution": "def sum_Of_product(n, algorithm_choice=None):\n    # This try-except block and the _has_math_comb flag are placed here\n    # to ensure all helper definitions are 'inline' with the function,\n    # as per specific instructions. This will cause re-evaluation on each call.\n    try:\n        from math import comb as _math_comb_internal\n        _has_math_comb_internal = True\n    except ImportError:\n        _math_comb_internal = None\n        _has_math_comb_internal = False\n\n    def comb(n_comb, k_comb):\n        \"\"\"Calculates the binomial coefficient C(n, k).\"\"\"\n        if k_comb < 0 or k_comb > n_comb:\n            return 0\n        if _has_math_comb_internal:\n            return _math_comb_internal(n_comb, k_comb)\n\n        # Fallback multiplicative algorithm for C(n, k)\n        k_comb = min(k_comb, n_comb - k_comb)\n        if k_comb == 0:\n            return 1\n        numer = 1\n        denom = 1\n        for i in range(1, k_comb + 1):\n            numer *= n_comb - (k_comb - i)\n            denom *= i\n        return numer // denom\n\n    def identity_formula(n_algo, comb_fn_algo):\n        \"\"\"Computes S(n) using the identity C(2n, n-1).\"\"\"\n        if n_algo == 0:\n            return 0\n        return comb_fn_algo(2 * n_algo, n_algo - 1)\n\n    def direct_using_comb(n_algo, comb_fn_algo):\n        \"\"\"Computes S(n) by direct summation of C(n, k) * C(n, k+1).\"\"\"\n        if n_algo == 0:\n            return 0\n        total = 0\n        for k_algo in range(0, n_algo):\n            total += comb_fn_algo(n_algo, k_algo) * comb_fn_algo(n_algo, k_algo + 1)\n        return total\n\n    # No input validation (e.g., isinstance, negative n) or error raising\n    # for invalid algorithm_choice, as per specific instructions.\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    DEFAULT_ALGORITHM = 'identity'\n    choice = algorithm_choice or DEFAULT_ALGORITHM\n\n    if choice == 'identity':\n        return identity_formula(n, comb)\n    elif choice == 'direct':\n        return direct_using_comb(n, comb)\n    # If algorithm_choice is invalid, no error is raised as per instructions,\n    # implicitly assume valid choice. For a truly complete function, an error\n    # for invalid choice would be appropriate.\n    return 0 # Default return for unhandled choice, assuming valid choices as per context."}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/599", "solution": "def sum_average(n) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Computes the sum and average of the first n natural numbers.\n    Raises TypeError if n is not an int or is a bool, ValueError if n is not positive.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or type(n) is bool:\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an int\")\n\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be >= 1\")\n\n    s = n * (n + 1) // 2\n    a = s / n\n\n    return (s, a)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def find_kth(A, B, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the k-th smallest element from two sorted arrays A and B.\n    Assumes k is 1-based and within valid bounds for the combined arrays.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Basic type and bounds validation\n    if not isinstance(k, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"k must be an int\")\n    try:\n        m, n = len(A), len(B)\n    except Exception:\n        raise TypeError(\"A and B must be sequence-like with __len__\")\n\n    total = m + n\n    if k < 1 or k > total:\n        raise ValueError(\"k is out of bounds for the combined arrays\")\n\n    # Handle empty array cases first.\n    if m == 0:\n        return B[k - 1]\n    if n == 0:\n        return A[k - 1]\n\n    # Ensure A is the shorter array for optimal binary search performance.\n    if m > n:\n        A, B = B, A\n        m, n = n, m\n\n    # Define sentinels for boundary conditions.\n    neg_inf = float('-inf')\n    pos_inf = float('inf')\n\n    # Binary search for 'i', the number of elements taken from A.\n    # 'left' is the minimum possible 'i', 'right' is the maximum possible 'i'.\n    left = max(0, k - n)\n    right = min(k, m)\n\n    while left <= right:\n        i = (left + right) // 2  # Number of elements taken from A\n        j = k - i                # Number of elements taken from B\n\n        # Determine the four elements that define the partition.\n        A_left_val = A[i - 1] if i > 0 else neg_inf\n        A_right_val = A[i] if i < m else pos_inf\n        B_left_val = B[j - 1] if j > 0 else neg_inf\n        B_right_val = B[j] if j < n else pos_inf\n\n        if A_left_val <= B_right_val and B_left_val <= A_right_val:\n            # Correct partition found, k-th element is the maximum of the left halves.\n            return max(A_left_val, B_left_val)\n        elif A_left_val > B_right_val:\n            # 'i' is too large, need to take fewer elements from A.\n            right = i - 1\n        else:  # B_left_val > A_right_val\n            # 'i' is too small, need to take more elements from A.\n            left = i + 1\n\n    # This point should not be reached if k is within valid bounds.\n    raise RuntimeError(\"Failed to locate k-th element\")"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/594", "solution": "def diff_even_odd(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the difference between the first even and first odd number in a list.\n    Returns first_even - first_odd (int).\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: if any element in lst is not an int.\n        ValueError: if no even or no odd number is found.\n    \"\"\"\n    first_even = None\n    first_odd = None\n\n    for elem in lst:\n        if not isinstance(elem, int):\n            raise TypeError(f\"All elements must be int, got {type(elem).__name__}\")\n        if first_even is None and elem % 2 == 0:\n            first_even = elem\n        if first_odd is None and elem % 2 != 0:\n            first_odd = elem\n        if first_even is not None and first_odd is not None:\n            break\n\n    if first_even is None or first_odd is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"List must contain at least one even and one odd integer\")\n\n    return first_even - first_odd"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/590", "solution": "import math\nimport cmath\n\ndef _validate_inputs(x_or_r: float, y_or_theta: float) -> None:\n    if not (isinstance(x_or_r, (int, float)) and isinstance(y_or_theta, (int, float))):\n        raise TypeError(\"polar_rect requires numeric inputs of type int or float\")\n\ndef _rect_to_polar(x: float, y: float) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    r = math.hypot(x, y)\n    if r == 0.0:\n        return (0.0, 0.0)\n    theta = math.atan2(y, x)\n    return (float(r), float(theta))\n\ndef _polar_to_rect(r: float, theta: float) -> complex:\n    return cmath.rect(r, theta)\n\ndef polar_rect(x_or_r: float, y_or_theta: float) -> tuple[tuple[float, float], complex]:\n    \"\"\"\n    polar_rect(x_or_r: float, y_or_theta: float) -> tuple[tuple[float, float], complex]\n\n    Compute both the polar representation of the inputs treated as rectangular coordinates\n    and the rectangular (complex) representation of the inputs treated as polar coordinates.\n\n    Parameters\n    - x_or_r (int | float): Interpreted as x when computing rectangular->polar, or as r when\n      computing polar->rectangular. Must be an instance of int or float. (Note: bool is a subclass\n      of int; per SRS this function accepts bool unless stakeholders explicitly require rejecting bool.)\n    - y_or_theta (int | float): Interpreted as y when computing rectangular->polar, or as theta (radians)\n      when computing polar->rectangular. Must be an instance of int or float.\n\n    Returns\n    - tuple: (polar_tuple, rectangular_complex)\n      - polar_tuple: tuple[float, float] == (r, theta) computed from rectangular inputs x = x_or_r, y = y_or_theta\n        where r = math.hypot(x, y) and theta = math.atan2(y, x). When x == 0 and y == 0, this function\n        returns (0.0, 0.0) for determinism.\n      - rectangular_complex: complex == cmath.rect(r, theta) computed with r = x_or_r and theta = y_or_theta\n\n    Raises\n    - TypeError: if either x_or_r or y_or_theta is not an instance of int or float.\n\n    Behavior and edge cases\n    - Zero radius: When x == 0 and y == 0, the rectangular->polar result is (0.0, 0.0). This removes\n      the angle ambiguity at the origin and is the deterministic behavior mandated by the SRS.\n    - Negative radius: For polar->rect conversion, negative r is allowed and follows cmath.rect semantics.\n    - NaN and infinities: Inputs that are NaN or infinite propagate according to IEEE-754 semantics; outputs\n      may be NaN or infinite. Callers and tests should use math.isnan and math.isinf to assert propagation.\n    - Boolean inputs: Since bool is a subclass of int in Python, bool inputs are accepted by the current\n      validation semantics; if bool must be rejected, BR-2 must be revised.\n\n    Numeric precision expectations\n    - Computations use IEEE-754 double-precision floating point (Python float). Small rounding errors\n      are expected; callers and tests should use math.isclose with appropriate rel_tol and abs_tol\n      values when comparing floats and complex components.\n\n    Examples\n    - Typical rectangular->polar:\n        >>> polar_tuple, rect = polar_rect(1.0, 1.0)\n        >>> # polar_tuple approximately (math.hypot(1.0,1.0), math.atan2(1.0,1.0))\n\n    - Typical polar->rectangular:\n        >>> polar_tuple, rect = polar_rect(1.0, math.pi/2)\n        >>> # rect approximately complex(0.0, 1.0)\n\n    - Zero radius:\n        >>> polar_tuple, rect = polar_rect(0.0, 0.0)\n        >>> polar_tuple == (0.0, 0.0)\n\n    - Negative radius:\n        >>> polar_tuple, rect = polar_rect(-1.0, 0.0)\n        >>> # rect follows cmath.rect semantics for negative radius\n\n    - NaN and infinities:\n        >>> import math\n        >>> polar_tuple, rect = polar_rect(math.nan, 0.0)\n        >>> math.isnan(polar_tuple[0]) or math.isnan(polar_tuple[1])\n\n    Notes\n    - The implementation must import math and cmath and use math.hypot, math.atan2, and cmath.rect\n      as required by the SRS.\n    - No I/O or global side-effects are performed.\n    \"\"\"\n    _validate_inputs(x_or_r, y_or_theta)\n    polar_tuple = _rect_to_polar(x_or_r, y_or_theta)\n    rectangular_complex = _polar_to_rect(x_or_r, y_or_theta)\n    return (polar_tuple, rectangular_complex)"}
